From f535bbfc8f484dfd39a9b451f3f59ef55007d32c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yang Chen <yang.chen@quantatw.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Jul 2025 14:47:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] PWMSensor: synchronize hardware PWM with D-Bus property

The Fan PWM sensor property setter (Value/Target) previously only
tracked the last set value (resp), but the actual hardware PWM
controller may reset to its own default value after a power cycle,
reset, or hot-plug event. If a client tries to set the same PWM value as
before, the setter compares only with resp and ignores the request,
leaving the hardware PWM unsynchronized with the D-Bus property.

This update changes the property setter logic to always compare the
requested value (converted to hardware PWM) with the actual hardware
PWM value read from the controller. If the hardware value differs from
the requested value, the hardware PWM is updated and the "resp" value is
always synchronized from the controller. This ensures the D-Bus property
and the actual hardware PWM remain consistent, even after controller
resets or power events.

Tested:
```
Test old code behavior on Meta Ventura platform:

Test target will not be set again with the same value after the
controller is reset

1. Set fan duty from target to 127
root@bmc:~# busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.FanSensor  /xyz/openbmc_project/control/fanpwm/FCB_0_FAN0_PWM_PCT xyz.openbmc_project.Control.FanPwm Target t 127

2. Get fan duty from dbus target property
root@bmc:~# busctl get-property xyz.openbmc_project.FanSensor  /xyz/openbmc_project/control/fanpwm/FCB_0_FAN0_PWM_PCT xyz.openbmc_project.Control.FanPwm Target
t 127

3. Read PWM value from driver
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
127

4. Use i2c command to reset the PWM controller
root@bmc:~# i2cset -y -f 18 0x20 0x00 0x60

5. Check that the PWM value is back to default 255 after reset
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
255

6. Set fan duty from target to 127 again
root@bmc:~# busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.FanSensor  /xyz/openbmc_project/control/fanpwm/FCB_0_FAN0_PWM_PCT xyz.openbmc_project.Control.FanPwm Target t 127

7. Check that the PWM value from the driver has not changed.
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
255

Test value will not be set again with the same value after the
controller is reset

1. Set fan duty from value to 50
busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.FanSensor  /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/fan_pwm/FCB_0_FAN0_PWM_PCT xyz.openbmc_project.Sensor.Value Value d 50

2. Read PWM value from driver
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
128

3. Use i2c command to reset the PWM controller
root@bmc:~# i2cset -y -f 18 0x20 0x00 0x60

4. Check that the PWM value is back to default 255 after reset
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
255

5. Set fan duty from value to 50 again
busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.FanSensor  /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/fan_pwm/FCB_0_FAN0_PWM_PCT xyz.openbmc_project.Sensor.Value Value d 50

6. Check that the PWM value from the driver has not changed.
root@bmc:~# cat /sys/class/i2c-dev/i2c-18/device/18-0020/hwmon/hwmon7/pwm1
255

